# User administration

Some operations are for trusted code only: creating users, suspending them,
changing [fields](/docs/auth/fields), [groups](/docs/auth/groups) and
[roles](/docs/auth/roles). None of them is exposed over HTTP, because only
your app knows who its administrators are.

Each takes `by:`, a `user.Actor`, which is recorded with the change:

- `user.Acting(principal)`: a signed-in user asked for this, such as an
  administrator using your admin page.
- `user.System`: code acting for nobody, such as a startup task or a
  scheduled job.
- `user.SystemFrom(client)`: the same, with where the request came from,
  for an operator request that arrived over a transport of your own.

`by:` records who acted. Deciding whether they were allowed is still your
job, and comes first:

```gleam
fn suspend_user(ctx, identity, permissions) {
  use admin <- guard.require(ctx, auth.required(identity))
  use _ <- guard.require(
    ctx,
    access.require_permission(permissions, "users.suspend", access.Global),
  )
  use user_id <- param.string(ctx, "id")
  auth.suspend(identity, user_id, by: user.Acting(admin))
  |> service.no_content(ctx)
}
```

## Creating users

`auth.provision(identity, email, by:)` creates a user without asking them:
an invitation, an import, a sync from a directory. It works with
registration turned off, which makes it the way to run an app that is by
invitation only.

```gleam
let assert Ok(ada) =
  auth.provision(identity, "ada@example.com", by: user.System)
```

Nothing is emailed. The user signs in with an email token when they are
ready, which is also what proves the address is theirs. An address that
already has an account is a `Conflict`.

`auth.provision_with` sets [fields](/docs/auth/fields) in the same
transaction. Outside `group.Single`, choose the group with
`auth.in_group(identity, group_id)`.

## Suspending

- `auth.suspend(identity, user_id, by:)` ends every session and cancels
  every emailed token of that user, in one transaction. They cannot sign in
  again until resumed.
- `auth.resume(identity, user_id, by:)` lets them sign in again. Old
  sessions do not come back.
- `auth.revoke_sessions(identity, user_id, by:)` signs a user out
  everywhere without suspending them.

A user that does not exist is a `NotFound`. `howdy/auth/users` has
`users.get` to look one up by id.

## The audit trail

Changes are recorded in the `howdy_auth_events` table, in the same
transaction as the change itself: the user affected, the action, the acting
user, the client, and a detail such as the role and scope or the sign-in
method.

Actions include `user.registered`, `user.provisioned`, `user.suspended`,
`user.resumed`, `user.moved`, `user.fields_changed`, `session.created`,
`session.revoked`, `sessions.revoked`, `password.set`, `token.requested`,
`login.failed`, `group.created`, `group.renamed`, `group.deleted`,
`role.defined`, `role.assigned` and `role.revoked`. A token request for an address that has an account is
recorded with its purpose, so you can answer somebody who asks why they
were sent an email.

Read the table with your own queries. There is no API for it yet, no
export, and nothing protecting it from a person with database access.

A client address is personal data. Decide how long to keep events, and
apply that from a scheduled job:

```gleam
auth.prune_events(identity, before: unix_seconds)
```

## Behind a proxy

Rate limits, password back-off and the audit trail all need to know which
client a request came from. By default that is the address of the socket,
which behind a reverse proxy is the proxy, for everyone. Tell both the
routes and the guard which header your proxy sets:

```gleam
import gleam/http/request
import gleam/option

let client = fn(ctx: controller.Context) {
  request.get_header(ctx.request, "fly-client-ip") |> option.from_result
}

routes.api_limited_by(identity, at: "/api/auth", key: client)
auth.required_from(identity, client)
```

Only read a header that your proxy overwrites. One the client can set lets
anybody choose their own bucket, and claim to be anybody in the audit
trail.

The route limits are 30 requests a minute per client to the endpoints that
send email, exchange tokens or check passwords, and 300 a minute to the
signed-in ones. They are kept in memory, so with several instances each
counts on its own. The per-address limits on
[email](/docs/auth/signing-in) and [passwords](/docs/auth/passwords) are
kept in the database, and are shared.

Your mail provider should cap the total volume of outgoing mail too.
Nothing here limits requests spread over many different addresses.

## Housekeeping

Expired tokens and sessions are removed as a side effect of sign-ins. On a
quiet installation, call `auth.prune_expired(identity)` from a scheduled
job. Nothing is scheduled for you.
